        "abstract": "<jats:p>The deployment of Agentic Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) systems in regulated enterprise environments-such as SAP landscapes, financial institutions, and healthcare networks-demands a paradigm shift from optimizing for accuracy alone to ensuring comprehensive trustworthiness. While Agentic RAG architectures demonstrate remarkable potential for automating complex, multi-step workflows, their adoption in high-stakes sectors is hindered by the absence of standardized, empirically validated evaluation metrics that capture the multidimensional nature of reliability, safety, and compliance. Existing benchmarks overwhelmingly prioritize response quality over critical dimensions such as audit log completeness, procedural fidelity, resilience to adversarial inputs, and deference mechanisms for uncertain scenarios. This research proposes a novel empirical evaluation framework specifically designed for trustworthy Agentic RAG in regulated settings. The framework introduces two primary metrics: Agentic Success Rate (ASR) , which measures the fidelity of end-to-end workflow execution against predefined business process standards (e.g., SAP S/4HANA transaction integrity), and Evaluation Completion Rate (ECR) , which quantifies the system's ability to reliably produce verifiable audit trails and defer to human experts when confidence thresholds are breached. Through a series of controlled experiments simulating SAP financial close processes, healthcare prior-authorization workflows, and FINRA-compliant trade reporting, we validate the framework's effectiveness. Preliminary findings demonstrate that our framework can identify performance degradation caused by vector store precision errors and RBAC granularity gaps-factors that conventional accuracy metrics miss entirely. The proposed evaluation methodology provides a blueprint for organizations to empirically certify Agentic RAG systems before production deployment, establishing a critical bridge between generative AI capabilities and the immutable audit, security, and interpretability requirements mandated by SOX, HIPAA, GDPR, and the EU AI Act. By shifting the evaluation lens from \"correctness\" to \"trustworthiness,\" this work aims to accelerate the responsible adoption of agentic AI across the most regulated sectors of the global economy.</jats:p>",
